---
import InlineCommand from "../../components/InlineCommand.tsx";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import TerminalBlock from "../../components/TerminalBlock.tsx";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Requirements Writing Exercise - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell chapterId="practical-exercise" subChapterId="requirements-writing">
    <div class="prose space-y-6">
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-800 mb-4">Exercise Overview</h2>
        <p>
            This task is centered around requirements gathering and requirements writing. You will move from analyzing existing (flawed) requirements to ideating and documenting a brand new feature.
        </p>
        <p>
          The goal is not just to produce a document, but to learn how to use AI to <strong>challenge your assumptions</strong>, <strong>identify edge cases</strong>, and <strong>structure your thoughts</strong> before writing a single line of code.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h3>Part 1: Requirements Analysis</h3>
        <p>
          We have provided a requirements document for a <strong>Habit Tracker</strong> feature. However, it intentionally contains missing details, ambiguities, and potential logical gaps. Without some of this info, the AI will struggle to implement the feature during the development phase.
        </p>
        <p>
          Your first task is to use the AI in "Ask" mode (or standard chat) to critically analyze this document. Think of this as <strong>AI rubber ducking</strong>: using the AI to audit the logic before development begins. It will be helpful to refer to the other requirement documents to point the AI to what is missing from the Habit Tracker requirements page.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg p-5 my-6">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-stone-800 mt-0 mb-3 text-sm">Task Instructions</h4>
          <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-700">
            <li>Open your terminal or chat interface.</li>
            <li>Reference the file <InlineCommand command="@docs/requirements/habit-tracker.md" client:visible /></li>
            <li>Ask the AI to identify edge cases, ambiguities, or missing technical constraints and fill these in where necessary.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
          <div class="bg-violet-50 border border-violet-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
            <div class="text-xs font-bold text-violet-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Example Prompts</div>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 leading-relaxed">
              <li class="text-sm text-violet-900">"Read @sample-project/docs/requirements/habit-tracker.md. Identify 3 edge cases that are not covered by these requirements."</li>
              <li class="text-sm text-violet-900">"Act as a senior QA engineer. Review the Habit Tracker requirements and list any ambiguities that might cause bugs during implementation."</li>
              <li class="text-sm text-violet-900">"What technical constraints or data model issues do you see in this document?"</li>
              <li class="text-sm text-violet-900">"If I were to implement this today, what questions should I ask the product manager first?"</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h3>Part 2: Feature Ideation & Requirements Gathering</h3>
        <p>
          Now that you've analyzed some requirements, it's time to gather them for a new feature. You will be designing a feature of your choice (or you may use the example provided).
        </p>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg p-5 my-6">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-stone-800 mt-0 mb-2">Example Feature: Unit Converter</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mb-0">
            A simple dashboard widget that allows users to convert between common developer units, such as <code>px ↔ rem</code> or <code>hex ↔ rgb</code>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          Before asking the AI to write the document, use it to <strong>flesh out the idea</strong>. This is the "Requirements Gathering" phase.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg p-5 my-6">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-stone-800 mt-0 mb-3 text-sm">Task Instructions</h4>
          <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-700">
            <li>Propose the feature idea to the AI.</li>
            <li>Ask the AI to help you identify the core implementation details (inputs, outputs, UI behavior).</li>
            <li><strong>Explicitly ask the AI to challenge you:</strong> "What am I missing? What makes this hard to build?"</li>
            <li>Refine the scope based on the AI's feedback.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
          <div class="bg-violet-50 border border-violet-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
            <div class="text-xs font-bold text-violet-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Example Prompts</div>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 leading-relaxed">
              <li class="text-sm text-violet-900">"I want to build a [<i>feature</i>]. Act as a skeptic Product Owner and ask me 3 difficult questions about why we need this and how it will work."</li>
              <li class="text-sm text-violet-900">"Reference @sample-project/docs/requirements/habit-tracker.md. Using that structure as a template, guide me through gathering the necessary details for my new feature."</li>
              <li class="text-sm text-violet-900">"What are the potential edge cases or error states for a [<i>feature</i>] that I might be overlooking?"</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h3>Part 3: Writing the Requirements Document</h3>
        <p>
          Once you have a clear understanding of the feature, and have described it adequately to the AI, use the AI coupled with the <strong>Requirements Writer</strong> skill to generate a structured requirements document for your new feature.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg p-5 my-6">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-stone-800 mt-0 mb-3 text-sm">Task Instructions</h4>
          <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-700">
            <li>
              Instruct the AI to generate a requirements file at <InlineCommand command="sample-project/docs/requirements/unit-converter.md" client:visible /> (or your feature name) using the requirement-writing skill. 
            </li>
            <li>Read over the generated document. Does it accurately reflect your discussion in Part 2? Review and make any changes you deem necessary.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
          <div class="bg-stone-100 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
            <div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Sample Prompt</div>
            <TerminalBlock
              title="generate requirements"
              lines={[
                { type: "command", command: "Use the requirements-writing skill to write a requirements document for the <feature> we just discussed. Save it to sample-project/docs/requirements/<feature-name>.md." },
              ]}
              client:visible
            />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="border-t border-stone-200 pt-8">
        <h3 class="text-stone-800 mt-0">Key Takeaways</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-3 mt-4">
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-stone-700 text-sm"><strong>AI as a Critic:</strong> Use AI to poke holes in your logic before you write code. It's cheaper to fix a requirement than a bug.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-stone-700 text-sm"><strong>Iterative Design:</strong> Don't expect the first draft to be perfect. Treat requirements gathering as a conversation.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-stone-700 text-sm"><strong>Structured Output:</strong> Always ask for structured deliverables (Markdown files, lists, tables) to make the output actionable. If you ever need to restart the context, having these structured outputs provides a useful record of the previous discussion.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
